Risk analyst at a UK bank interpreting provider data in the financial performance and condition review.

**Concept and theory**

The financial performance and condition review asks whether the provider can afford to keep delivering the service for the full contract term. If the provider fails financially, the institution faces disruption and the operational, reputational and legal consequences of an interrupted service. GARP lists the elements the review may include:

- the financial condition of the provider and its closely-related affiliates;
- the most recent financial statements and annual report, with regard to outstanding commitments, capital strength, liquidity and operating results;
- sustainability, including the length of time the provider has been in business and its growth of market share for a given service;
- the potential impact of the institution’s business relationship on the provider’s financial condition;
- the provider’s commitment, both financial and staff resources, for the duration of the contract;
- the adequacy of the provider’s insurance coverage;
- the adequacy of the provider’s review of the financial condition of any subcontractors;
- other current issues that could affect future financial performance.

